Materials. Poly (3-hexylthiophene) (P3HT) and (6,6)-phenylC61-butyric acid methyl ester (PCBM) were bought from Luminescence Technology Corp. and Nichem Fine Technology Co., Ltd., respectively. 1-Pyrene butanol, 4-(dimethylamino) pyridine (DMAP), and nano graphite powder were purchased from Aldrich, Alfa Aesar, and Aladdin, respectively. The glacial acetic acid, concentrated sulfuric acid, concentrated hydrochloric acid $(30\%),N,N^{\prime}$ -dicyclohexylcarbodiimide (DCC), sodium methylate, $\mathsf{K}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{8},\mathsf{K M n O}_{4},\mathsf{P}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{5},\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{2},$ aqueous ammonia, and hydrazine hydrate were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co. Ltd., China. Poly (3,4-ethylenedioxythiophene):polystyrene sulfonic acid (PEDOT:PSS, Baytron P) was purchased from SCM Industrial
Chemical $\mathsf{C o.}$ Ltd. All the reagents were directly used as received without further purification unless specially mentioned.
Characterization. $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR spectra were recorded on a Bruker AV400 NMR instrument using tetramethylsilane (TMS) as an internal reference. FTIR spectra were recorded on a TENSOR 27 spectrometer (Bruker, Germany) at room temperature by dropping samples onto KBr pellet. UV vis absorption spectra were measured on a UV-3600 spectrometer (Shimadzu, Japan) using a quartz cell of $10~\mathsf{m m}$ layer thickness and 1 nm resolution. Raman spectra were taken at room temperature with a LABRAM-HR equipment (Jobin Yvon, France) operating at a wavelength of $514.5~\mathsf{n m}$ as the excitation source, and the samples were dropped onto silicon substrates and dried into a film. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was conducted on an
ESCALAB 250 (Thermo-VV Scientific) instrument. A JEOL JSM7401F instrument (Japan) was used for SEM imaging. The atomic force microscopy (AFM) images were taken using a Nanoscope III (Digital Instruments) operating in height mode by dropping samples on new cleaved mica and dried in atmosphere. Scanning Kelvin probe microscopy (SKPM) measurements were carried out on AFM using the standard SKPM mode. A surface profilometer (KLA-Tencor P6) was used to measure the film thickness.
Synthesis. The synthetic route of the new graphene fullerene composite (rGO-pyrene-PCBM) is shown in Scheme 1. The detailed synthetic procedures are as follow:
Synthesis of [6,6]-Phenyl-C61-butyric Acid 1-Pyrene Butyl Ester (Pyrene-PCBM). [6, 6]-Phenyl-C61-butyric acid (PCBA) as the carboxylated PCBM was synthesized according to the literature method.3335 Briefly, PCBM $(75\mathrm{\mg},0.082\mathrm{\mmol})$ ) was dissolved in $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ of toluene $(0.82\mathrm{mmol}{\cdot}\mathsf{L}^{-1}.$ ) under heating at $110^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ with stirring for $2\ h$ then glacial $C H_{3}C O O H$ $(50~\mathrm{mL})$ ) and diluted HCl $\boldsymbol{20}\mathrm{mL}$ ) were added. The mixture was stirred and refluxed for $12\mathsf{h}.$ , and the reaction was monitored by TLC (silica/toluene). All the volatile components were then removed in vacuum and the residue was transferred into a glass centrifuge bottle and treated with methanol and toluene to precipitate the product. The residue was dried in vacuum at $60^{\circ}C,$ affording $50~\mathrm{mg}$ of PCBA. PCBA $(50~\mathrm{mg},0.056~\mathrm{mmol}$ ) was redissolved in a mixture solvent of ${\mathsf C}{\mathsf S}_{2}$ and $C H_{2}C l_{2}$ (1:1 v/v). 1-Pyrene butanol $(62~\mathsf{m g},$ $0.23\:\mathrm{mmol}$ ) was added then into the mixture, and ultrasonicated for 3 min to achieve a homogeneous solution. The mixture was stirred at room temperature with ${\mathsf N}_{2}$ protection for $30\mathrm{min}$ . Then $N,N^{\prime}$ -dicyclohexylcarbodiimide (DCC) (14 mg, 0.068 mmol) and dimethylamino pyridine (DMAP) (2.7 mg, 0.022 mmol) dissolved in $3m L$ of $C H_{2}C l_{2}$ were added into the mixture, stirring for 3 days. During the reaction, the mixture turned clear and then orchid color. After the reaction, the mixture was concentrated to $5~\mathrm{mL}$ through $N_{2}$ blowing, then centrifuged at 9000 rpm for 3 min to precipitate the unreacted PCBA and DCC hydrate produced during the reaction. The resultant mixture was purified by column chromatography (CC): $\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ $(100-200)$ and 200 300 mesh) and dried under vacuum $(27.5~\mathsf{m g})$ ). $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR (300 MHz, $C D C\vert_{3},$ ) $\delta$ (ppm): 8.25 (dd, $\scriptstyle J=9.3,$ , 3.7 Hz, 1H), 8.18 8.07 $(\mathsf{m},4\mathsf{H})$ , 8.03 7.94 (m, 3H), 7.88 7.80 $(m,3H)$ ), 7.51 7.37 (m, 3H), 4.15 (t, $J=6.3~\mathsf{H z},$ 2H), 3.38 (dd, $\ J=14.9_{\L}$ , 7.5 Hz, 2H), 2.93 2.79 (m, 2H), 2.51 (t, $J=7.2$ Hz, 2H), 2.22 2.08 (m, 2H), 1.99 1.87 (m, 2H), 1.81 (dt, $J=11.9,6.3\mathsf{H z},2\mathsf{H}.$ ).
Preparation of Chemically Reduced Graphene Oxide (rGO) and Graphene Fullerene Composite (rGO-Pyrene-PCBM). GO was synthesized from nano graphite powder by a modified Hummers method.39 Chemically reduced graphene oxide (rGO) was prepared through the modified method reported by Li and co-workers.40 Briefly, the asprepared homogeneous $N,N$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) dispersion of GO $(2.0~\mathrm{mL}$ , $1.6\:\mathrm{mg/mL}$ ) was mixed with $14.0~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF, $50~\mu\mathsf{L}$ of hydrazine solution (85 wt $\%$ in water), and aqueous ammonia (28 wt $\%$ in water, the amount of ammonia solution was monitored by $\mathsf{p H}=10)$ in a three-necked bottle. After vigorously shaking or stirring for a few minutes, the three-necked bottle was placed in an oil bath $(\sim95^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ for $1\mathrm{~h~}$ . After reduction, a homogeneous black dispersion with a small amount of black precipitate was obtained. The resultant mixture solution was centrifuged at 9000 rpm for 15 min to remove any visible precipitate. The reserved liquid was further centrifuged at 9000 rpm for $10\mathrm{\min,}$ and a stable, homogeneous black dispersion of rGO without any precipitate was obtained. To prepare for the dispersion of graphene fullerene composite (rGO-pyrene-PCBM), $2m L$ of pyrenePCBM solution in DMF $(1.6\:\mathrm{mg/mL}$ , $1.39\times10^{-3}\mathrm{mmol}{\cdot}\mathsf{L}^{-1};$ ) was added into the GO dispersion before the additions of hydrazine and ammonia, followed by the reduction procedure described above. A homogeneous dispersion of rGO-pyrene-PCBM without any macroscopic precipitate was achieved and a further centrifugation at 9000 rpm could not yield any precipitate either. Washing the dispersion of rGO-pyrene-PCBM by toluene and ${\mathsf C}{\mathsf S}_{2}$ did not generate any dissolved fullerene moiety.
BHJ-PSC Device Fabrication and Measurements. Our detailed fabrication procedure of the P3HT:PCBM BHJ-PSC devices has been reported previously.34,5053 Briefly, the ITO-coated glass substrate (8Ω/0, purchased from Shenzhen Nan Bo Group, China)
was first cleaned with detergent, then ultrasonicated in acetone and 2-propanol, and subsequently dried in vacuum at $60~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ overnight. PEDOT:PSS (Baytron P) layer $(\sim35\mathsf{n m}$ thick) was spincoated onto the ITO substrate and then annealed at $120^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . The P3HT:PCBM $(1{:}0.8~\mathrm{w}/\mathrm{w})$ blend was dissolved in chlorobenzene by stirring at $40~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ until all the materials dissolved, which was spin-coated onto the PEDOT:PSS layer affording the P3HT:PCBM active layer $(\sim65\ \mathsf{n m}$ thick). To prepare for the device with rGO-pyrene-PCBM incorporated as electron extraction layer, rGO-pyrene-PCBM was dispersed in the mixture solution of DMF and 2-propanol with a concentration of ${\sim}0.35~\mathrm{{mg/mL}},$ and then spin-coated onto the P3HT: PCBM active layer at 3000 rpm for $60~\mathsf{s}$ . All of the solution processing and film preparation were carried out in air atmosphere. The device was then transferred into vacuum chamber $(\stackrel{\cdot}{\sim}10^{-5}$ Torr), and an Al electrode $(\sim~100~\mathsf{n m}$ thick) was deposited on the top of the active layer through a shadow mask to define the active area of the devices $(2\times5~\mathsf{m m}^{2})$ . Finally, thermal annealing was carried out at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for 10 min on a digital hot plate under a nitrogen atmosphere inside a glovebox.
PCE was measured under simulated AM 1.5 irradiation (100 $\mathsf{m}\mathsf{W}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ ) using a standard xenon-lamp-based solar simulator (Oriel Sol 3A), for which the illumination intensity was calibrated by a monocrystalline silicon reference cell (Oriel P/N 91150 V, with KG-5 visible color filter) calibrated by the National Renewable Energy Laboratory (NREL). The current voltage $\left(J-\boldsymbol{V}\right)$ characteristics were measured with a Keithley 2400 source meter. All the measurements were carried out in air atmosphere and a mask with well-defined area size of $10.0\ \mathrm{\mm}^{\dot{2}}$ was attached onto the cell to define the effective area so as to ensure the accurate measurement. More than 10 devices were fabricated independently under each experimental condition and measured to ensure the consistency of the data, and the average results were used in the following discussions.
